{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-37900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and $f(-1)=-3,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-37900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=f(-1)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-37900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e68ffc0592b5173f19eec8fd8a738ece46f250905202b668efdbf39634415e0c"} {"question" "q-37900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-37900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-37900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-48106" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ -x, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-48106" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- $f(1)=1,$ so $f(1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x) \\\\  & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-48106" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e68ffc0592b5173f19eec8fd8a738ece46f250905202b668efdbf39634415e0c"} {"question" "q-48106" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-48106" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)"] ["text" "$f(1)$ does not exist"] ["text" "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function"] ["text" "${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] ["text" "${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-48106" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-104148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-\\sqrt{3}?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{3}, & x<-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3, & x=-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+3}, & x>-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-104148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- $f(-\\sqrt{3})=3,$ so $f(-\\sqrt{3})$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+3}{3} \\\\  & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)=2.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)=2,$ while $f(-\\sqrt{3})=3.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)\\ne f(-\\sqrt{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-104148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e68ffc0592b5173f19eec8fd8a738ece46f250905202b668efdbf39634415e0c"} {"question" "q-104148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-104148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}f(x)"] ["math" "{lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)\\ne f(-\\sqrt{3})"] ["text" "$f(-\\sqrt{3})$ does not exist"] ["text" "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function"] ["text" "${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)$ does not exist"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-104148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)\\ne f(-\\sqrt{3})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-104153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=5$ and $f(5)=2,$ then what is the value of\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-25f(x)}\\,?\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-104153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=5.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 5}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5}f(x)=f(5)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-25f(x)} & =\\sqrt{{5}^{3}-25\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-104153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e68ffc0592b5173f19eec8fd8a738ece46f250905202b668efdbf39634415e0c"} {"question" "q-104153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-104153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "10\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "5\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-104153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-104155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and $f(4)=-6,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(f(x))^{2}}{6x}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9ebd28fbaf7e9c0db8af827a6cfb8f8f"} {"question" "q-104155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4)=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(f(x))^{2}}{6x} & =\\frac{(-6)^{2}}{6\\cdot 4}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987348/state.json" "file_sha256" "83dade9cbe5d04d70090ab9efc73e1bf9eb